  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 AM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nAs low pressure settles along the coast for the next several\ndays, we will see multiple \"ripples\" swing around through during\nthat stretch. We have one of those ripples crossing this state\nthrough tonight. The highest chances through late\nafternoon/early evening will be across the central mountains\nsouth through the Magic Valley and South Hills/Albion Mountains.\nBesides the potential for some locally heavier rainfall, there\nis a 50-90% chance of for gusts over 35 mph with the strongest\nstorms. High end gusts up to 45 mph are possible over most of\neastern Idaho, with a corridor of gusts approaching 60 mph\npotentially across the Magic Valley, Lower Snake Plain, and \nSouth Hills/Albion Mountains. Through this evening and\novernight, the better chance of showers and storms will exists\nnorth of a Shoshone to Driggs line. Gusty winds and a good\ndownpour are possible with storms, even past midnight. The focus\nquickly shifts to the central mountains tomorrow. We have a\nslightly stronger \"ripple\" racing northeast. As drier air works\nin across most of eastern Idaho, enough moisture and proximity\nto the low itself will keep a decent chance of rain and\nthunderstorms across the central mountains. It is possible a\nstorm or two develops across the eastern highlands, but that is\nstill under 20%. Gusts over 35 mph with storms in the central \nmountains remains possible, although timing suggests the\nstrongest winds develop across western Montana. The main impact\nwill increasing wind across the rest of eastern Idaho. \nSustained winds of 15-30 mph with higher gusts are expected, \nwith a fairly narrow corridor of sustained 30-35 mph with gusts \ncloser to 45 mph from Craters to Monida Pass. We decided to NOT \nissue a WIND ADVISORY for tomorrow due to the localized coverage\nof highest winds, but that may need to change based on trends \nover the next 6-12 hours. We will briefly reach LAKE WIND \nADVISORY for American Falls Reservoir, but again we will opt of \nthat for now based on the limited timeframe. For Thursday, the \npattern really doesn't change much from tomorrow. Any potential \nfor showers and storms is confined to the central mountains. \nSome higher resolution models want to spread that into portions \nof the Magic Valley and also eastern highlands, but will let the\ncurrent Blend of Models forecast ride. It will be breezy again,\nslightly \"weaker\" than Wednesday. The pattern will bring in \ncooler conditions, especially for the central mountains. Higher \nvalleys will only be in the 60s after today, with lower valleys \nrunning in the 70s to near 80. \n\nThis pattern really doesn't budge until we get to Sunday and\nbeyond. In fact, we may see enough drier air that only low\nchances of showers and storms occur across the central mountains\nFriday and especially Saturday. We do see a small rebound in\ntemperatures both days, with some low 80s returning for some\nvalley locations. The forecast gets a bit tricky for early next\nweek. There is a window where we do see a surge of monsoon\nmoisture, including tapping into what's associated with current\nTropical Storm Marie. The ECMWF and associated models, as well\nas the GEFS and AIGFS, have a shorter window where we see that\nsurge of moisture vs. the main GFS model. All of the models,\nhowever, keep low pressure over the western U.S. despite any\ntype of interaction with Marie. This should drop highs back into\nthe 60s and 70s during that period.",
